# Response headers, served by Cloudflare. Copied into dist/ by the Astro build
# like everything else in public/, and read from the root of the deployment.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE REASON THE SITE MOVED OFF GITHUB PAGES.
#
# Pages sends `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` on everything
# and gives you no way to change it. That is defensible for HTML and wrong for
# the 7 MB of hero frames, which never change and are requested 68 times a
# visit. Every rule below is about that.
#
# Syntax: a path pattern on its own line, then indented `Header: value` lines.
# First matching block wins per header, so the specific paths come before /*.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- immutable ---
#
# These three are safe to cache forever, but for two different reasons and it
# is worth knowing which is which.
#
# /_astro/* is content-hashed by Astro: a change produces a new filename, so the
# old URL can be cached forever because nothing will ever need to replace it.
#
# /hero/* and /fonts/* are NOT hashed — they are at fixed paths on purpose. The
# font has to be preloadable from the HTML, which means its URL must be known
# before the build, and the hero frames are fetched by a script that builds
# their URLs from a frame number. So `immutable` here is a promise the build
# makes rather than a fact the filename guarantees: regenerating them with
# `npm run hero` or `npm run fonts` reuses the same URLs, and a visitor holding
# the old bytes will keep them for a year.
#
# That is the right trade — the frames are a finished render and the font is a
# pinned package — but it is a trade. **If the hero is ever re-rendered in a way
# that must reach existing visitors, the fix is a new path, not a shorter
# max-age.** Bump the directory (`/hero2/`) or add the manifest's `generated`
# date to it; do not quietly drop this to an hour and make every first-time
# visitor pay for a change almost nobody needs.
/_astro/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/hero/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ a while ---
#
# Generated but replaceable at the same URL, so they get a week rather than a
# year. resume.pdf is rebuilt from cv.json on every deploy and is the one file
# here a visitor might be handed a stale copy of that actually matters — a week
# is short enough that a corrected résumé reaches anyone who looks twice, long
# enough that it is not re-fetched on every visit. The OG cards are the same
# shape of problem with lower stakes; social platforms cache them far longer
# than we ask anyway.
/resume.pdf
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/og/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/og.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- html ---
#
# Everything else, which in practice is the HTML. Deliberately revalidated on
# every request: a page is the one thing that must never be stale, and it is
# also the cheapest thing on the site to re-fetch. `must-revalidate` plus a
# CDN-side cache means Cloudflare still serves it from the edge and only asks
# the origin whether it changed.
#
# The security headers sit here rather than on a narrower path because they
# should apply to every response, including the ones added later by someone who
# has not read this file.
#
# No Content-Security-Policy yet, and its absence is deliberate rather than an
# oversight. The site runs three inline scripts that cannot be removed — the
# theme resolver and the hero's activation script both have to execute before
# first paint, and the JSON-LD block — so a useful policy needs per-script
# hashes generated at build time. The contact form also POSTs to
# api.web3forms.com and fetches it from script, so `form-action` and
# `connect-src` have to name it. That is a real change with a real way to get
# silently wrong (a broken form fails quietly, on the page a client uses), so it
# belongs in its own commit with the form tested after it, not smuggled in here.
/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), interest-cohort=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
